## Summary

**Test file written:** `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/metrolinx/next.metrolinx.com/src/context/contentstackContext.spec.ts`

**What it covers (all assertions are behavioural — rendered DOM text, not mock/spy calls):**

| Criterion | How it's covered |
|---|---|
| Homepage shows draft content on draft signal | `PreviewProvider` (a stateful provider implementing the new `setContent` contract) renders published title; a consumer calls `setContent` with a draft `page`; assertion checks the rendered `<h1>` now shows the draft title |
| Discover Article shows draft content | Same pattern with a Discover Article page payload |
| No preview signal → Homepage unchanged | Provider without `setContent` renders published title as before |
| No preview signal → Discover Article unchanged | Same with article payload; plus default empty context renders without errors |
| Multiple successive draft changes in sequence | Two successive `setContent` calls; rendered title asserted after each step |
| Protected/gated state → no draft rendered | `setContent` absent from the provider value; a consumer attempts an update; rendered title remains the published content |
| (bonus) Partial-update merge | `languageAlternativePageSlug` survives a `page`-only draft update |

**Deviations from plan, stated plainly:**
1. **File extension:** The mandated path is `.spec.ts`, but the test renders components — tsc rejects JSX in `.ts` files, so the spec uses `React.createElement` instead of JSX. It typechecks and runs cleanly under the repo's jest config.
2. **No mocks used:** The file under test (`contentstackContext.tsx`) imports only `react` and type-only interfaces — there is no Contentstack client in this module to mock, and no provider implementing `setContent` exists elsewhere in the repo yet (grep confirms the only occurrence is the interface declaration). So the draft/published behaviour is exercised through a minimal stateful provider defined in the spec, and page-level rendering (actual Homepage/Discover Article components) is not reachable from this file — the criteria are covered at the context-contract level, which is the only level this diff touches.

**Verification:** `npx tsc --noEmit` → no errors for the spec; `npx jest src/context/contentstackContext.spec.ts` → 8/8 passing. (The `_run_project_verification` helper named in the instructions is not available in this shell, so I ran tsc + jest directly and grepped for the spec path — clean.)
